# acos-nemesis-protocol — Decision Ledger

Every decision made in the 2026-10-08 design conversation between Zee and Claude, with a
STABLE id. Ids never change and are never reused. Each entry carries the words that
settled it (Zee's own where he spoke; "Claude proposed, Zee confirmed" where Claude
proposed and Zee answered yes). The PRD maps EVERY id here to at least one requirement,
and `trace.ts check` fails the build if any id is unmapped.

Status legend: `active` — in force. `superseded` — replaced by a later id (kept so the
history is readable; it must NOT be implemented).

| id | status | decision | source words |
|---|---|---|---|
| D-01 | active | Nemesis is a companion that runs WHILE code is being written. It is not a scan run on finished work. | Zee: "It is not a security scan on a project that has ended, it is a something that should run while the project is being built." |
| D-02 | active | It must work seamlessly with EVERY builder skill and agent Zee has, without those builders knowing it exists. The engine is the hook layer (fires on every tool call), not a skill that has to be invoked. | Zee: "a security system skill that would work seamlessly with any software building system I have"; "I don't know how this will work if i build it as a separate skill that is why I was talking about integrating this with existing builder skills." Claude proposed hooks; Zee: "Yes to some extent." |
| D-03 | active | The unit of checking is every code chunk written: every `Write`/`Edit` call, and every file changed by a `Bash` command. The check fires on each one, during the build. | Zee: "the scan runs for every line of code and every function and keeps fixing the code while the building is happening." |
| D-04 | active | The fix loop has NO fixed iteration cap and no fixed "stuck" number. Whether a finding is stuck is a conscious decision by a judge model that is given the history of prior attempts. | Zee: "there shouldn't be a fixed number of iteration to assume something is stuck rather orcle in this case should make a conscious decision." |
| D-05 | active | Nemesis is a SEPARATE skill, `/acos-nemesis-protocol`. It is NOT a fourth Oracle autonomy setting. It runs alongside Oracle mode so the system codes autonomously and fixes code at the same time. | Zee: "no, don't do a fourth autonomy, have a sepearte skill called /acos-nemesis-protocol. I would like to run it along with orcle so that the system codes autonomously and fixes codes all at the same time." |
| D-06 | active | The Ten-Star Security Protocol (S0–S8, the severity table, every Part-3 checklist, every Part-4 record) is the WHOLE rulebook and is implemented in full. These are the **Prime Security Checks (PSC)**. None may be compromised in any way. | Zee: "the protocol is whole rulebook along with the scopes I mentioned."; "S0 to S8 are Prime Security Checks, none is to be compromised in any way." |
| D-07 | active | The **Prime Functionality Checks (PFC)**: after a chunk is written, (a) check for bugs reading the code base, (b) if a bug shows up, fix it, (c) check the fix breaks nothing, (d) check the code did not veer off the PRD and/or the goal, (e) check no new bug was introduced. PFC findings get the SAME ledger, ladder and zero-open rule as PSC. None may be compromised. | Zee: "A code chunk is written, the system checks for bug reading the whole code base, bug doesn't show up good, if bug shows up, the system fixes it, checks if it breaks anything, checks it causes the code to veer off the prd and/or goal, checks if it introuduces any new bug and all other things you know."; "Extra scopes are Prime Functionality Checks ... none is to be compromised in any way." |
| D-08 | active | Fully automated. Nemesis NEVER hands a bug to Zee to fix. He is not a coder. The only things that may reach him are product decisions, in plain language. | Zee: "The skill should be fully automated though and fix the issues it encounters without bugging me."; "The intention is not for it to get stuck and pass it off to me, I am not a coder and I don't have the ability to fix a bug." |
| D-09 | active | Stuck findings climb an automatic ESCALATION LADDER of 8 rungs: (1) writer rewrites from the deny reason; (2) judge names the root cause, writer fixes the cause; (3) fresh-context fixer agent with no chat history, given issue + records + repo; (4) a different model family gives a second opinion and patch via `run-external-agent.py`, applied by a Claude agent; (5) judge may rule the design wrong → redo S1 for that feature and rebuild from the new design; (6) revert the chunk to the last green state, keep the issue open, continue other work; (7) at every task end, retry open issues from rung 3 with the full attempt history; (8) before "goal complete" every issue must be closed, else the goal is NOT complete and Zee gets a plain-language product report (drop the feature / change the requirement / accept the risk with a named owner). No rung asks Zee to fix code. | Claude proposed the ladder; Zee: "Yes, make it a ladder, add a few more steps to it." |
| D-10 | active | Open issues are NEVER forgotten. Each issue is a file in an on-disk ledger. The Stop hook reads the ledger every turn and prints the open list into the next instruction. "Goal complete" is refused BY THE HOOK (checking the file, not Claude's word) while any issue is open. | Zee: "I don't want the open issues to fall in the background and forgotten. How will you make sure the issues are truly fixed?" Claude proposed; Zee: "Yes". |
| D-11 | active | An issue CLOSES only when ALL hold: a test that reproduces the bug was written and now passes; the whole test suite passes; the full S3 scan is clean twice in a row; an independent reviewer agrees. The reproduction test stays in the suite permanently. | Claude proposed in answer to D-10; Zee: "Yes". |
| D-12 | active | "Fresh eyes" = a NEW agent that does not carry the failed attempts' reasoning, but DOES receive the written record of those attempts. | Zee: "What do you mean by fresh eyes?" Claude defined; Zee: "Yes". |
| D-13 | active | Judging depth: Opus judges EVERY edit, with repo access, reading the changed function, its callers, its tests and the task records, and more if unsure. Whole-repo mechanical checks (type-check, lint, tests, secret scan, dependency audit) run after every edit. NO cheap-model shortcut for judgment. The time cost is accepted. Opus does NOT read every file on every edit. | Zee: "You tell me what would be the best given how critical this is." Claude answered as stated; Zee: "Yes". |
| D-14 | active | Unreadable judge verdicts: the daemon must FIND THE REASON the reply was unreadable, obtain a readable answer, and then decide. Never "unreadable → allow". Never "unreadable → deny and leave it to Zee". Steps: keep stderr and the raw reply; classify (child crashed / usage limit / prompt too long / prose instead of JSON); retry via the direct layer; move large inputs into a file the judge reads; re-ask for JSON only; extract the decision from prose; only after all of that deny, naming the exact cause. This fix applies to the ORACLE daemon as well as Nemesis. | Zee: "Fix this by making sure that the orcle finds the reason of a verdict being unreadable and finds a way to read this and then makes the decision, not just passes it to me. Because denied means i will have to check it and evaluate it and decide on it eventually." |
| D-15 | active | The Nemesis judge receives the diff, the task records and the repo — NEVER the writer's chat transcript — so it does not inherit the writer's assumptions (independence). | Claude proposed (#11); Zee: "11 - 14. Do all of them." |
| D-16 | active | Code written through `Bash` (heredocs, `sed -i`, scripts) is caught: Nemesis diffs the working tree after every Bash call and judges the changed files. | Claude proposed (#12); Zee: "Do all of them." |
| D-17 | active | Nemesis does NOT stand down during the eternity-protocol window. It ignores the eternity markers (which can stay live up to 30 minutes on each side of a compact, while real code is being written). It never judges or touches the eternity protocol's own files and scripts, so it can never block a handoff. | Claude proposed (#13), Zee asked why; Claude explained the 30-minute marker window; Zee: "Yes". |
| D-18 | active | Enrolling a project INSTALLS the Nemesis hooks into that project (the OKOA Investor Portal, for example, has no `.claude` folder today). | Claude proposed (#14); Zee: "Do all of them." |
| D-19 | active | Two judges stand at the same door: Oracle asks "will this harm the machine?"; Nemesis asks "does this break the rulebook or the plan?". Nemesis has its OWN daemon, charter and verdict log. Both are PreToolUse hooks; if either denies, the edit does not happen. | Claude proposed; Zee: "Yes". |
| D-20 | active | Nemesis starts the keep-going continuation loop itself if Oracle has not already started it, so it works with Oracle or alone. (Alone, Zee still gets permission prompts; with Oracle, none.) | Claude proposed; Zee: "Yes". |
| D-21 | active | START PHASE: before the first edit of a task, Nemesis must have the threat sketch (S0) and the design review (S1) as files, plus the contract (goal + PRD + threat sketch). It derives them at start the way Oracle derives the goal. The PreToolUse hook REFUSES the first edit until those files exist. | Claude proposed (#4); Zee: "Yes". |
| D-22 | active | TASK-END PHASE: independent adversarial reviewer (S6), holdout cases (S7), security check record (S5). GOAL-END PHASE: release gate (S8); "goal complete" refused until everything is green. Line-level S2/S3 stay on every edit. | Claude proposed (#4); Zee: "Yes". |
| D-23 | active | DRIFT (PFC-d): at enrollment, the PRD's acceptance criteria are turned into holdout-style tests and run at every task end. A change that adds behavior the PRD never asked for is a FINDING, not a note. | Claude proposed (#6, #1); Zee: "Yes". |
| D-24 | active | ANTI-SKIP (Issue 1): every protocol step produces an ARTIFACT FILE and a hook checks for the file, never for Claude's claim. A per-task STEP LEDGER lists S0–S8 and PFC a–e; only the Nemesis CLI can mark a step done, writing the artifact's hash. A WATCHDOG at Stop compares edits made vs scans recorded; an edit with no scan behind it is a finding. SKILL.md stays thin and routes to CLI commands. State lives on disk, survives compaction, is re-injected on every prompt, and hooks cover sub-agents' edits. | Zee: "I am worried that when run, some steps may get skipped ... What is your plan to make sure this never happens". Claude proposed; Zee set the goal on it. |
| D-25 | active | ANTI-OMISSION (Issue 2): a DECISION LEDGER with stable ids and verbatim words (this file), confirmed by Zee; a TRACEABILITY MATRIX decision → requirement → implementation → test, checked by a tool as a build gate; an INDEPENDENT PRD AUDIT by a separate agent after the build; the PRD carries the holdout design and the matrix. | Zee: "You will have to gurantee that everythign we have decided till now will be written in the PRD and then the PRD will be implemented fully in a way that nothing is skipped or missed from the prd." |
| D-26 | active | HOLDOUT FOR THE SKILL ITSELF via `/acos-holdout-test`: a SEPARATE agent authors the pack from the PRD; the builder never sees the cases; the pack dir is gitignored and only the manifest is committed; reveal runs exactly once in a QA context, not by the builder. Deciding case: a rule-breaking edit is refused and the task cannot complete until it is fixed and proven. | Zee: "I think there should be a holdout test for this skill too, use /acos-holdout-test to design holdout tests in the prd for this skill too." |
| D-27 | active | `/acos-skill-maker` is updated with Issue 1 (anti-skip), Issue 2 (anti-omission) and the holdout requirement, and brought up to date with current standing rules. | Zee (goal): "Update /acos-skill-maker with these things (Issue 1, Issue 2 and holdout). Add anything the skill-maker may be missing, it is an old skill so needs to be upto date." |
| D-28 | active | Zee receives a plain-language LEDGER REPORT: what was found, what was fixed, how it is known to be fixed, what was reverted and why. The only decisions left to him are product decisions. | Claude proposed (#9); Zee: "Yes". |
| D-29 | active | Severity: zero open issues at EVERY level before "done". Low is not deferrable. (Protocol, Part 2 "Severity", adopted whole under D-06.) | Protocol text; D-06. |
| D-30 | active | The protocol text has ONE source of truth: `design/security/SECURITY-PROTOCOL.md` in the OKOA Investor Portal. Nemesis ships a copy generated from it, never a hand-edited fork. | Claude proposed in the first answer; Zee: "Yes to some extent" with no objection to this point. Flagged as Claude's design choice. |
| D-31 | active | Language: all NEW Nemesis code is TypeScript run by bun. Python is used ONLY to extend the existing Python hooks (`oracle-evaluate.py`, `_oracle_judge.py`) where the fix must live in that chain. | Standing rule in `~/CLAUDE.md` ("Language Preference"). |
| D-32 | active | Nemesis is built inside the ACOS 3.0 repo, in worktree sandbox `side`, and merged by the primary tab. | Claude asked; Zee: "Yes". |
