| D-35 | active | Every FIX is itself a code chunk and goes through the same gate as the code it fixes. | Claude: "every fix is itself a code chunk that gets checked"; Zee: "Yes". |
| D-36 | active | Nemesis must not read `review-rules/` (Independence Wall) and must not modify `.claude/agents/` without human approval — ACOS restricted files stay restricted. | ACOS CLAUDE.md "Restricted Files". |
| D-37 | active | Nemesis records are append-only and are the evidence: every verdict, every issue, every step mark is on record; the S5 record is assembled from them. | Protocol "Everything on record"; Claude noted verdicts.log fits; Zee: "Yes". |
| D-42 | active | LANES — who clears a finding. A finding about NEMESIS'S OWN RECORDS (a missing or stale S0/S1, an edit with no S2 verdict, a skipped S3 check, a holdout/review record, anything whose only files are under `.nemesis/`) is MACHINE WORK and may never reach Zee. There is nothing in it for a product owner to drop, change or accept. The release gate stays shut until the record is repaired, so no check is weakened — only the owner changes. A finding under a rule about code (`3.x`, `Qn`, `PFC-a..e`) stays a product finding even when its wording complains that the sketch is stale, because the underlying risk is still a risk. **SHARPENED 2026-11-02 (same day):** the lane follows the place the judge POINTS AT (`where`), not whether every file it lists is under `.nemesis/`. A finding pointing at `.nemesis/holdout/R-019/manifest.json:35` while also naming `_build/headings.test.ts` had come back `product` and reached Zee. | Zee, 2026-11-02: "The intention was for nemesis to make the correct decisions based on the understanding of the complete picture. If the decision comes to me, I will not know everything and the complete context."; "fix the skill so that these problems don't happen again." Measured: of 356 findings sent to him, 104 were about Nemesis's own paperwork. |
| D-43 | active | RUNG 8 ADMISSION — an answerable question. Rung 8 offers exactly three choices: drop the feature, change the requirement, accept the risk with a named owner. A finding may reach Zee ONLY if those are answerable for it, which needs two anchors on record: a FEATURE he could drop (the task's title) and a REQUIREMENT he could change (the task's `prd_refs`). A finding missing either is not a product decision; supplying the anchor is machine work and the refusal names the command. | Zee, 2026-11-02, as D-42. R-024 as written said the gate "moves EVERY open issue to rung 8" with no admission test of any kind. |
| D-44 | active | RUNG 8 REQUIRES AN EXHAUSTED LADDER. The position rungs (7 at task end, 8 at goal end) may no longer promote a finding that has not been through the machine's own options. Before a finding reaches Zee it must carry a recorded attempt at rung 2 (root cause), rung 3 (fresh-context fixer) and rung 4 (second opinion from a different model family) — or, for each of those, a recorded judge ruling naming why that rung cannot apply. This is the direct repair of D-08: the machine must spend its own ideas before spending Zee's attention. **WIDENED 2026-11-02 (same day):** the required rungs are 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 - every rung the MACHINE owns - not a subset. With only 2, 3 and 4 required, the machine ruled rung 2 inapplicable 345 times and rung 3 inapplicable 151 times with 123 distinct written reasons, and 356 findings reached Zee anyway; its own reasons named rung 5 as the route it should have taken. Ruling a rung out must move along the ladder, never shorten it. And being PARKED at a rung is not an attempt at it: task end writes "7 -> 3", which had been counted as rung 3 spent on 345 of 396 findings. | Zee, 2026-11-02, as D-42, and D-08: "The intention is not for it to get stuck and pass it off to me". Measured on the website-design-okoa/v2 ledger: of 356 open findings, **0** had ever been moved up a rung by the judge, **0** had ever reached rung 4, 5 or 6, and **83** went from rung 1 straight to Zee in one hop. |
| D-45 | active | GROUPED, NOT ENUMERATED. When findings do reach Zee they arrive GROUPED by feature and requirement, with one decision covering every finding in the group, and the group's own three choices stated once. A per-finding enumeration is not a decision he can make. | Zee, 2026-11-02: "I don't even know what i need to decide." Measured: the product report he was handed was 517,926 bytes across 356 sections. |
| D-46 | active | THE HOOK MUST NOT LOOP. When the ledger is genuinely with Zee, the Stop hook allows the turn to end, says so once, and does not block again until the ledger changes. It keeps TWO counters and releases on either: identical directives repeated (repeating cannot help) and consecutive blocks in one Stop chain, read from Claude Code's `stop_hook_active`. Both caps sit below Claude Code's own cap of 8, so NEMESIS always releases before the host overrides it — an override ends the turn with none of the hook's own record of why. The chain counter is the one that matters: on the measured ledger the watchdog opened a fresh finding almost every turn, so the directive CHANGED every turn and a counter keyed on the text would have reset each time and reached 8 anyway. A release is never a pass: it says the goal is NOT complete and names what is owed and by whom. | Zee, 2026-11-02: "fix the skill so that these problems don't happen again." Measured: the hook blocked 9 consecutive turns twice (2026-11-01T08:00:00.431Z, 2026-11-02T06:16:08.457Z) and Claude Code's own cap (`CLAUDE_CODE_STOP_HOOK_BLOCK_CAP ?? 8`) had to override it; the stalled escape re-armed instead of latching, firing 5 cycles and writing 16 duplicate reports totalling 8.9 MB. |

| D-47 | active | A HOLDOUT PACK IS STAGED, SEALED FROM THE STAGING COPY, AND MOVED INTO PLACE ONLY WHEN THE SEAL SUCCEEDS. A refused seal deletes the staging copy and leaves the previously sealed pack and its manifest exactly as they were. Nemesis's own author also pre-checks that every expectation carries the key its grade mode is read for, as a second and earlier line of defence. A reveal that produced no verdict is reported as a PACK defect naming the missing keys, never as "acceptance behavior not proven". | Measured in that project's own `holdout.jsonl`: FIVE `seal-failed` events — one for a bad `status` value, four for "manifest already exists — re-sealing changes the commitment". On every one the NEW files had already overwritten the previously sealed pack while `manifest.json` still committed to the OLD hashes, so the directory no longer matched its own manifest. CORRECTION, 2026-11-04: an earlier version of this row claimed four packs on website-design-okoa/v2 could not grade themselves. That was WRONG — it read a snapshot taken while the window was re-authoring, so `cases.json` and `expected.json` momentarily came from different generations. All four carry real verdicts (R-045 PASS, R-050 BLOCK, R-052 BLOCK, R-056 BLOCK), and the sealer has refused ungradable packs since 2026-10-31T05:53:55Z. |
| D-48 | active | A REPRODUCTION TEST IS CREDITED ONLY ON POSITIVE EVIDENCE: the named test must be DECLARED in the file the reproduction points at, and must not be skipped or todo, with the whole suite green. "Nothing said it failed" is not evidence that anything ran. The evidence is read from the FILE rather than the suite output, because `bun test` prints only "1 pass / 0 fail" on success and names a test only when it fails — measured, so demanding a pass marker in the output would have blocked closure on every project using it. A refusal names its cause so the machine can act on it. A dynamically named test is credited when a name PATTERN in the file can produce the registered name (a template-literal title with `${...}` holes, or an `.each` title with printf placeholders); a pattern that cannot produce it is still refused, with its own message. | Found by the v2-ee session on 2026-11-04 in this skill's own `lib/scan.ts`: credit required the FILE to exist, the suite to pass, and no failure line to mention the test NAME. A name present nowhere produces no failure line either, so it was credited. FUN-93 on website-design-okoa/v2 carried a reproduction pointing at an unrelated test and still satisfied closure criterion (1), which is the one check that proves a bug is actually fixed. | Audited on that project 2026-11-04: all 94 reproductions pass the new rule (81 closed, 4 decided, 10 open), so nothing was reopened. The dynamic-name clause was raised by the v2-ee session: none existed there, but a literal-only rule would eventually refuse a real reproduction — the same class of false refusal as requiring a pass marker in `bun test` output.
| D-49 | active | CHECKS AND BALANCES ON THE MACHINE'S OWN JUDGEMENTS. D-44 gave the machine an escape so the ladder could never deadlock: the judge may rule a rung inapplicable with a written reason. That escape had no check on it, and it became the new front door. Three guards, all enforced in code: **(a) THE FLOOR** — at least one required rung must be GENUINELY ATTEMPTED, never only ruled out. An all-N/A ladder is not an exhausted ladder, and `nemesis second-opinion <id>` always provides one real rung (it records `unavailable` with the reason when no provider is configured), so this can never deadlock. **(b) INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION** — a ruling lands as `rung-na-proposed` and counts for nothing until a second reader, which never saw the first judge's reasoning (D-15), agrees the rung is IMPOSSIBLE rather than merely unpromising. The confirmer is told its job is to DEFEND the ladder, so its default answer is that a rung can be attempted. An unobtainable confirmation leaves the ruling recorded and uncounted. **(c) A RECURRING REASON BECOMES A POLICY** — a reason already used on `na_reason_cap` findings (default 3) is refused, and must be written as a POLICY instead: one readable sentence, a matcher, the rung its class takes, the reasoning, the EVIDENCE that justified it, and a test naming a case it must fire on AND a near-miss it must not. A policy applies to nothing until an independent reader approves it, every application is on record, and Zee can veto any policy by id — which walks back every finding it moved. **THE INVINCIBILITY LINE: a policy may choose only among rungs 2-6, the ones the machine owns. It can never close, decide, drop, accept or rule N/A, and the `Policy` type has no field that could express any of those, so the guarantee is structural rather than a check that can be forgotten.** D-11's four closure criteria still hold for every finding, every time. A policy makes the machine FASTER at spending its own options; it can never make the gate LAXER, and it can never shorten the ladder — only move along it. | Zee, 2026-11-04: "Just using the judgement is not enough, it has to be best judgement with checks and balances. that was one of the main purpose of the nemesis protocol. The codes writed needs to be invincible. so make sure that happens." Measured on website-design-okoa/v2: the D-44 escape was used 496 times (345 on rung 2, 151 on rung 3) with 123 distinct written reasons — roughly four rulings per reason, so the same judgement was re-derived from scratch again and again, reviewed by nobody, remembered by nothing, and 356 findings reached Zee anyway. 83 of them went from rung 1 to Zee in a single hop. | Measured consequence, 2026-11-04, in two rounds. (i) The floor immediately refused three of this skill's own selftest fixtures, which had been reaching Zee with zero of rungs 2-6 genuinely spent; they were amended to spend one for real rather than the floor being loosened — the fixtures were wrong, not the floor. (ii) **The floor as first written was nearly useless, and the live ledger said so within minutes.** It counted rung 4 as spent the moment `second_opinion` existed, INCLUDING `status: "unavailable"`, so one auto-recorded non-event was the whole ladder for 243 findings. Measured on website-design-okoa/v2 (cut stated exactly, because the `v2-ee` session could not reproduce a bare "338" and got 400 on a different one): of 447 open findings, **400** carry `unavailable`, **338** of the **376** in the PRODUCT lane carry it, **47** have no `second_opinion` key at all, and **ZERO** anywhere carry `received` — no external model is configured on that project. The 243-of-266 figure below is on the product-lane basis, the same basis as the 266. The floor now counts REAL WORK only (`rungSpentOnRealWork`): an attempt the judge saw, a second opinion genuinely RECEIVED, a redesign, or a revert. The owed list still accepts `unavailable`, so nothing deadlocks. **Admitted to Zee on the live ledger: 266 → 23**, and all 23 carry real work (12 redesigns, 6 fresh-agent attempts, 3 root-cause fixes, 2 both). The `v2-ee` session reproduced the 23 independently by importing the live `lib/lane.ts` and calling `admitToRung8` itself, with the same ids and the same split (247 floor / 106 owing a rung / 71 record lane). (iii) **Two consequences of the tighter floor, both found by that session and both fixed.** An attempt was stamped with `issue.rung`, but rungs 7 and 8 are POSITIONS, so work re-judged while a finding was parked at 8 recorded `rung: 8` and paid for no required rung — real work credited to a step that is not work (3 such stamps exist in the wild; all 130 floor-refused findings on the task in hand sit at position 8). `workRungFor` now maps a position to rung 3, which is not invented: rung 7's own instruction is "Retry from rung 3 with the FULL attempt history". And the refusal text named rung 3 as "available on anything", which is false as a PAYMENT: `recordFindings` appends an attempt only when the finding's task matches the task being worked, so on the 117 floor-refused findings belonging to other tasks a fresh agent could do real work and nothing would record it. There is no deadlock, so the floor stands: `cmdTaskRedesign(taskId, issueId, file)` and `cmdRevert(id)` take the target explicitly with no rung gate and no current-task gate, so rungs 5 and 6 are payable on any finding. The refusal now names the route that will actually be credited and says how to make rung 3 payable. |
| D-50 | active | ONLY A FUNCTIONALITY OR DESIGN CHANGE REACHES ZEE — the gate tests the KIND of question, never the count. Three parts. **(a) A SECURITY finding (lens PSC) can never be a question for him, at any rung.** His standing answer covers every one of them, and there is nothing left to ask; coding the security is the system's job. It cannot be decided, and it cannot even be CLAIMED as his. **(b) Every other finding needs a POSITIVE, RECORDED, INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED claim** that no code change can clear it and the remaining question is about what the product DOES or how it LOOKS: `nemesis issue product-question <id> --kind <functionality|design> --why "..."`, confirmed by a reader that never spoke to the claiming judge. That reader is told its job is to DEFEND THE OWNER'S ATTENTION, that its default answer is "machine work", and that a bug, a crash, a wrong result, a contradiction between two documents, a stale count or date, a comment disagreeing with its code, and anything it is unsure about are ALL machine work however often they recur. A refused claim is permanent — re-claiming cannot change it. An exhausted ladder is no longer a reason to ask him. **(c) "ACCEPT THE RISK WITH A NAMED OWNER" IS DELETED.** It existed only so he could sign off a lapse he has ruled he will never sign off. Rung 8 now offers TWO choices: drop the feature, or change the requirement. The KIND test runs LAST, after D-42/D-50(a)/D-43/D-44/D-49, so a refusal always names the machine's next MOVE rather than a claim to make. | Zee, 2026-11-04, verbatim: "Still, I am not a programmar. Now it is 23, if I do a large enough job, it could become 230. Do you see what I am saying. Other than when it comes to design, that I can see with my own eyes, no coding decision should come to me unless I specifically want some change be made. I care about functionality and security. As long as a system functions the way I want and there is no security laps in any way, I am good. Only questions about any change in functionality and design should come to me, nothing else. It is the systems job to code to ensure the functionality and the security." | **Why a count could never have fixed it, which is his point.** D-49 had taken the live ledger from 266 to 23. Of those 23: 2 were security, and of the 21 others roughly twelve were one document contradicting another ("the edit changes 'seventeen tests' to 'eighteen tests', but the same note still says Eigh…"), and the rest were code bugs or code-versus-design mismatches. **None of the 23 was a functionality or design change.** A gate keyed on volume would have let all 23 through and 230 through on a job ten times the size; a gate keyed on KIND lets through only as many as the PRODUCT has genuine change questions, which does not scale with how much code was written. **Measured after the change, on website-design-okoa/v2: of 464 open findings, 0 reach Zee** — 74 Nemesis's own paperwork, 46 security, 221 with nothing yet tried, 102 still owing a rung, and 21 that cleared every other gate and are refused precisely because they are not functionality or design questions. Removing `accept` broke nothing: of 88 decisions ever recorded there, 88 were `drop` and 0 were `accept`. The release gate is strictly TIGHTER, not looser — a secret finding used to be acceptable, which took it off the open list; now it must actually be fixed, and R-034 still blocks the release until a rotation is recorded even once it is closed. |
| D-51 | active | A RE-AUTHOR NEVER SPENDS THE OLD COMMITMENT BEFORE THE NEW ONE EXISTS. Both re-author paths - `nemesis task holdout <id> --new` and `nemesis task redesign` - renamed `manifest.json` to `.spent-<ts>.json` BEFORE calling `authorTaskHoldout`. That function never throws; it RETURNS a string, and several of its exits are refusals ("NOT authored", "NOT sealed"). On any of those the task was left with a pack directory and NO manifest, so the graded verdict of the pack actually committed to became unreachable. Both callers now go through `reauthorHoldout`, which spends the manifest, authors, and puts the old manifest BACK if no new one exists afterwards - including when authoring throws. A successful re-author still spends the old manifest, which is R-033's behaviour unchanged. | Found 2026-11-04 by watching the `v2-ee` session work around it by hand: *"I copied the graded manifest and pack aside first, per the rule that --new spends the manifest before it authors."* A workaround a careful operator has to remember is a defect, not a rule. | This is the SAME defect as D-47 - a destructive step taken before a step that can fail - and D-47 fixed it one layer down, inside the sealer, while leaving it standing in both callers. The lesson: **when a defect is "destructive step before fallible step", fix every caller, not just the layer where it was found.** Measured by selftest R-069: with authoring forced to refuse, the manifest is byte-identical afterwards, no orphan `.spent-` file is left, and the restore is both said out loud and recorded as `reauthor-refused`. |
| D-52 | active | A DERIVED FILENAME COMES FROM THE HIGHEST NUMBER ALREADY USED, NEVER FROM A COUNT. `cmdTaskRedesign` named the archive it keeps of the outgoing design `design-review.v<COUNT+1>.yaml`, counting the files that match the pattern. That collides the instant anything is removed or renamed: with v1..v15 present, count+1 is 16 and is safe, but delete v3 and count+1 becomes 15, which already exists, and `copyFileSync` overwrites it without complaint. A stored prior design is a permanent record - `steps.json` holds the sha256 of the S1 artifact that was actually installed - so losing one is unrecoverable. The number now comes from `Math.max(...existing) + 1`, and the copy REFUSES outright if that name somehow exists. Separately, a design must not hand-type a version number it does not control; the command now WARNS when the opening line carries one, and never blocks. | Found 2026-11-04 answering a question from the `v2-ee` session: why do `design-review.v15.yaml` and `design-review.v14.yaml` both open `# S1 v14 - ...`? Measuring the live R-019 archive to answer it: SIX of the sixteen archived designs name the wrong version - v5, v6, v7, v8 and v5-namespace all say "S1 v4", and v15 says "S1 v14". Counting also ignores `design-review.v5-namespace.yaml`, which already exists and matches no pattern the counter sees. | The peer's reasoning for leaving the archived headers alone is CORRECT and is now the rule: never edit a stored prior design, because `steps.json` pins its sha256 and the edit would make the record disagree with what was installed. The header is a symptom; the defect is that the name is DERIVED while the header is TYPED, with nothing tying them together. The generalisable part: **a count is not an identity.** This is the third time in this protocol that a count stood in for the thing it was counting and decayed - D-44/D-49 (a count of findings as the admission gate), D-50 (the KIND must gate, not the count), and now a count as a filename. Measured by two selftests: with v1 present and a v3 planted, the next archive is v4 and the planted v3 is byte-identical afterwards (the old rule would have overwritten it); and a design whose first line reads `# S1 v9` draws the warning while a plain one draws none. The overwrite refusal itself is unreachable by construction and is kept as a belt, not a tested branch. |
| D-53 | active | A RESTORE THAT ONLY RUNS IN-PROCESS DOES NOT COVER A KILL. D-51's restore lives in a `catch` and in a post-return existence check; a SIGKILL reaches neither. The manifest has already been renamed to `.spent-<ms>.json`, so the task is left with a pack directory and NO commitment - and with nothing in the records either, because `appendRecord` never ran. `reauthorHoldout` now writes `reauthor.inflight.json` naming the exact file to put back BEFORE the destructive rename, and `recoverInterruptedReauthor` puts it back on the next command. Ordering is the whole guarantee: killed between marker and rename, the manifest is still there and recovery is a no-op; killed after, the marker names the one file to restore. Recovery renames a spent file back ONLY when no `manifest.json` exists, so it can never overwrite a live commitment, and it is gated on the marker's pid being dead. | Raised 2026-11-04 by the `v2-ee` session, which noticed its OWN standing rule - kill the author the moment a design sentence is found false, applied three times that day - deliberately triggers the single case the tool did not cover. R-019 on website-design-okoa/v2 was in exactly that state: 19 `.spent-*.json` files and no `manifest.json`, so nothing could tell which of the 19 had been the live commitment. | THE TRAP, and it is the reason the obvious mitigation is wrong: restoring the manifest BEFORE killing is destructive. The sealer refuses when a manifest is present - `acos-holdout-test/scripts/holdout.ts:106` throws *"manifest already exists at <path> - re-sealing changes the commitment. Pass --force only to fix an UNSPENT pack you authored."* - and `authorTaskHoldout` passes no `--force`. So a manifest put back under a still-living author turns a run that was about to SUCCEED into one that REFUSES. The order is KILL FIRST, THEN RESTORE, and nothing is lost that way because spent files are never deleted. The generalisable part: **a recovery path that lives in the same process as the thing it recovers from is not a recovery path.** Measured by selftest R-071, which reproduces the exact post-kill state rather than simulating a refusal. KNOWN LIMIT, stated because it matters: this prevents the next occurrence and CANNOT repair R-019, which was killed before any marker existed. A commitment is never guessed. |
| D-54 | active | A RELEASE MUST NOT RESET A COUNTER THE HOST IS STILL COUNTING. The Stop hook bounds itself below the host's `CLAUDE_CODE_STOP_HOOK_BLOCK_CAP ?? 8` with `block_chain`, driven by `stop_hook_active`. TWO branches zeroed or dropped that counter while the host's chain was still live: the cap release wrote `block_chain: 0`, and the stalled-writer branch omitted the field entirely so `idle.block_chain ?? 0` read 0. Either way the hook restarted its own count from 1 while the host kept counting, so a release let exactly ONE turn through and blocking resumed. The release now KEEPS the count, which makes it sticky for the rest of the chain, and the stalled branch carries it across untouched. When the host's chain truly ends, `stop_hook_active` goes false and the count falls back to 1 on its own, so blocking resumes normally. | Found 2026-11-09 from the live records on website-design-okoa/v2: blocks at chain 2,3,4,5,6, a release at 7 (`identical-block`), then chain 1,2,3,4,5 again, with `stop_hook_active` TRUE on every one. The host reached nine and printed *"A hook blocked the turn from ending 9 consecutive times - overriding and ending turn."* | THE REASON THE EXISTING TEST MISSED IT: it ran exactly `HOST_CAP` turns, and the second run of blocks begins after that window closes. The new test runs `HOST_CAP * 2` turns in ONE unbroken chain. It also had to be written carefully: a stalled-writer turn is ALSO a non-block, and asserting "never blocks again after any non-block" would assert a guarantee this hook does not make. The test identifies the cap release by its own words and asserts the guarantee the host actually measures - the longest consecutive block run stays under `HOST_CAP`. Measured: the sequence went `BBBBB-BBBBB-BBBB` before and `BBBBB-B---------` after. |

## How to amend

A new decision gets the next id (D-38 …). A changed decision keeps its id, moves to
`superseded`, and a new id states the replacement. Never edit a row's words after Zee has
confirmed it. Run `bun .claude/skills/acos-skill-maker/scripts/trace.ts check <TRACE.json>`
after any change — an unmapped active id fails the gate.

## Corrections to the EVIDENCE columns (the decisions themselves stand)

A decision's words are never edited once confirmed, so a measurement that turns out to be wrong
is corrected here, additively, with the row left visible.

- **D-44's evidence is WRONG where it says "0 had ever been moved up a rung by the judge, 0 had
  ever reached rung 4, 5 or 6".** Re-measured 2026-11-04 on the same project: of the 278 open
  findings at rung 8, **132** had been promoted by the judge at least once and **99** had passed
  through rung 4, 5 or 6. `records/ladder.jsonl` holds **212** judge promotions — 88 of them
  `1 → 5` — the earliest dated 2026-10-31T02:12:56.172Z and **202 of them before** the
  2026-11-02 fix. So the figure was wrong when written, not merely stale. The DECISION it
  supports is unaffected: requiring rungs 2-6 is right for its own reasons, and the real leak
  was the N/A escape (1,503 rulings on open findings by 2026-11-04, up from 496), which is what
  D-49 addresses. The lesson is the one already written down for the digest trap and walked past
  anyway: **a measurement that returns zero on a system that is visibly working is a broken
  measurement, not a finding.**

## Appended during build (2026-10-08, Claude's design choices — strike any you do not want)

| id | status | decision | source words |
|---|---|---|---|
| D-38 | active | TEST MODE: a stub judge (canned verdicts from an env var) is honored ONLY when the project's `.nemesis/config.json` has `"test_mode": true` AND the env var is set. `enroll` never writes `test_mode`. Needed so the holdout pack and selftests can exercise hooks without a live Opus call; designed so it cannot become a bypass in a real project. | Claude's design choice, not yet ruled on by Zee. |
| D-39 | active | GREEN SNAPSHOTS: after every clean S3 the CLI snapshots the in-scope files that changed since the last snapshot into `.nemesis/snapshots/`; rung 6 revert restores from the last snapshot. Git history is never rewritten and no commits are made by Nemesis. | Claude's design choice (how "revert to last green" is realised). |
| D-40 | active | S0/S1 are COMPOSED by the writer (the planning side) and VALIDATED + RECORDED by the CLI (required fields, six STRIDE lines, non-empty checklists, abuse case). The judge later checks the sketch against the diff, so a dishonest sketch is itself a finding. | Claude's design choice (who writes the threat sketch). |
| D-41 | active | STALLED WRITER: if the writer makes no tool call for `stalled_turns` consecutive turns (default 6) while the ledger is open, the Stop hook writes the rung-8 product report, records `stalled` on every open issue and lets the session stop. The goal is NOT complete and the issues stay open; the next `on` resumes them. Blocking forever would burn tokens and fix nothing. | Claude's design choice (follows from "the intention is not for it to get stuck" and "never hands code fixes to Zee"). |
